"""LDhybrid v1.0 -- hybrid2 limb-darkening coefficients in MLX, MPS-ATLAS Set 2 (plane-parallel; Kostogryz et al. 2022), JWST_NIRSpec_PRISM passband.

MLX port of ldhybrid_hybrid2_mps2_nirspec_prism.py. Needs only mlx and numpy; the coefficient grid is
embedded. After import every function is MLX ops,
so the coefficients can be batched over any shape, differentiated with respect
to the stellar parameters (mx.grad, mx.vjp) and compiled (mx.compile) inside a
model:

    import mlx.core as mx
    from ldhybrid_hybrid2_mps2_nirspec_prism_mlx import coefficients, intensity
    r = coefficients(mx.array([5600.0, 5650.0]), 4.4, 0.0)
    r = coefficients(5600.0, 4.4, 0.0, correction="B100G")
    r["coeffs"]      # (..., 2): (w1, w2); also r["w"]
    r["q"]           # (..., 2): (q1, q2), sampling coordinates in [0, 1]
    r["inside"]      # (...): inside the grid? (outside: NaN, or the nearest model)
    r["physical"]    # (...): positive and monotonic?
    intensity(r["coeffs"], mu)                  # I(mu)/I(1), shape (..., len(mu))
    from_q(q), to_q(c)                          # unit hypercube <-> coefficients

The weights are on the hybrid2 shapes Elómë and SquishierPlanet take:
with Elómë, limb_dark="hybrid2" and u = r["w"] (flux_dev_hybrid takes
batched (n, 2) weights); Elómë's hybrid2_from_q samples the same triangle.

Law (hybrid2):
    I(mu)/I(1) = 1 - w1 (1 - mu^2) - w2 P(mu; e)
    P(mu; e) = [(mu^2 + e)^-2 - (1 + e)^-2] / [e^-2 - (1 + e)^-2],  e = 0.36
The positive, monotonic hybrid2 profiles are exactly the triangle with
vertices O, V_c = (-0.124615, 1.124615) and V_l = (1.201039, -0.201039) (VERTICES). Sampling
coordinates: w = sqrt(q1) [(1 - q2) V_c + q2 V_l]; uniform q is a uniform
prior over the triangle. to_q keeps the sign of the depth in q1, so weights
outside the triangle give q outside [0, 1].

Coefficients: least squares uniform in projected radius (the transit-optimised
fit), constrained to that triangle. Grid: 16974 models, Teff 3500-7500 K,
log g 3.5-5.0, [M/H] -2.0 to +1.0.
lc_ppm is the light-curve error of the tabulated fits (p = 0.1).

Interpolation is linear on the Kuhn simplices of the (Teff, log g, [M/H])
tensor grid, as in ldhybrid_hybrid2_mps2_nirspec_prism.py and ldhybrid.interpolate: continuous and
piecewise linear in the stellar parameters (so the gradient is piecewise
constant), exact at the models, and a convex mix of four models (the law's
physical region is convex, so a mix of physical models is physical).
Outside the grid the outputs are NaN and inside is False, with no exception,
so a call can be traced; extrapolate=True uses the nearest model (in
Teff/100 K, log g/0.5, [M/H]/0.5) there instead, with zero gradient.

Precision follows the inputs, as in Elómë: float32 (mx.arrays or numpy) is
computed in float32 on the default device; anything else (Python floats,
float64) in float64 on the CPU stream, which matches ldhybrid_hybrid2_mps2_nirspec_prism.py
to 1e-12. float32 agrees to about 1e-6 in the coefficients. mx.grad with respect to a float64
mx.array must itself be called under mx.stream(mx.cpu) (an MLX rule). MLX
evaluates lazily, so outputs that are never used (such as physical) cost
nothing.

correction: None (default) or a MURaM surface-field state, "ssd" (small-scale
dynamo), "B100G", "B200G", "B300G". For FGK stars LDhybrid recommends
MPS-ATLAS Set 2 + "B100G" (matches Kepler/TESS hosts); quiet Sun-like stars
prefer "ssd". MURaM covers 3196-6807 K at [M/H] = 0.
Generated by LDhybrid scripts/make_standalone.py from the v1.0 CDS tables.
The pole is the v1.0 value; check that any other hybrid2 implementation
uses the same one before passing these weights to it.
"""

